{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass at evening, and the quail came up, etc., and in the morning there was a layer of dew.</b> The quail and the manna began to come on the first of the week, and the quail lasted for them forty years just as the manna lasted. And just as it is written (Exodus 16:35): ״ובני ישראל אכלו את המן ארבעים שנה״ — 'and the children of Israel ate the manna forty years' — so too with the quail, that they ate it for forty years: this came by day and that came by night. But because the wonder was exceedingly great in the manna, since a new creation was created for them in heaven, therefore Scripture mentions this wonder alone, that it lasted for them forty years. But the quail, which came to them as though by way of nature and happenstance, in the manner of wind — as it is explained in another place (Numbers 11:31): ״ורוח נסע מאת ה׳ ויגז שלוים מן הים״ — He did not bother to mention it." "<b>And it appears</b> to me that because the quail came in the way of the world, as though it were in nature, therefore Scripture says of it ותעל השלו — 'and the quail came up' — and did not say 'and it was' (ותהי), but rather ותעל, like the language of (Exodus 8:2): ״ותעל הצפרדע״. But concerning the great wonder of the descent of the manna, which was an emanation of the supernal light, Scripture uses the language of הויה (coming-into-being): ״ובבקר היתה שכבת הטל״ — 'and in the morning there was a layer of dew' — for thus you find the language of הויה in the work of Creation regarding the supernal light (Genesis 1:3): ״יהי אור ויהי אור״." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> How did the manna descend? A north wind would go out and sweep the dust from the face of the earth, and afterward the dew would descend and level the surface of the ground, and afterward the manna would descend upon it. For it is not written here ״ויעל המן״ — 'and the manna came up' — but rather ״ותעל שכבת הטל״ — 'and the layer of dew came up' — teaching that the dew would descend from the heavens and cover it, so that there was dew above and dew below, and it was covered as though placed in a box. And according to the Midrash the verse is interpreted thus: ״ובבקר היתה שכבת הטל סביב למחנה״ — 'and in the morning there was a layer of dew around the camp' — this is the dew from below, in order to level the surface of the ground; ״ותעל שכבת הטל״ — 'and the layer of dew came up' — this is the dew from above, which would descend upon the manna after the manna was already upon the face of the wilderness, ״דק מחספס דק ככפור״ — 'fine, scale-like, fine as frost.'" "<b>And from this</b> you have cause to reflect and understand the words of our Rabbis of blessed memory, who said: 'one spreads a cloth and recites the blessing of sanctification' — and their intention is to place upon the table two cloths, one above and one below, with the bread in the middle, as a remembrance of the manna, which had dew above and dew below with the manna in the middle. And Rashi of blessed memory explained ״ותעל שכבת הטל״ — 'and the layer of dew came up' — to mean: when the dew that lay upon it rose up toward the heat of the sun, then it was revealed; this is what it says: ״והנה על פני המדבר דק מחספס״ — 'and behold, upon the face of the wilderness, fine, scale-like.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:00:10.428437075Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}